{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj3PSggmWtnSttUWFKZa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016ewt12m8KokKK1WQJea2aE","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"Start: G clef with three sharps. Then a sharp before four beamed tremolo groups drawn with three beams. A sharp is needed for D#, so these are D#5. Each group looks like an eighth's worth of 32nds, so the four together make the two D#5 quarter tremolos of bar 34. After them come two figures, each an 8th, a 16th rest and a 16th, with small notes written above and below. These are bar 34's E5 8th, rest, D#5 16th and E5 8th, rest, F#4 16th; the second voice's E5 matches the extra note heads. A barline follows, so bar 34 is complete. Next, in the middle of the line, there are two more 8th–rest–16th figures. One has a sharp (D#5) and one dips low to F#4. Then come two 8th-plus-8th-rest pairs. This is bar 35: G#4 8th, rest, D#5 16th; E5 8th, rest, F#4 16th; G#4 8th, rest; G#4 8th, rest. A second barline follows. At the end: an 8th, an 8th rest, then a run of 16th-like notes. These are the start of bar 36 (G#4 8th, rest 8th, G#4 16th, G#4 8th, G#4 16th tied, …). The line breaks partway through that bar, and the next line finishes it. So the line holds bars 34 to 36, not only bar 35. Small differences: the copyist abbreviates the tremolos as beamed groups, and the 32nd-note count can't be checked exactly.","right":false,"insights":"Two clear barlines make this line easy to split into three bars, and two details pin bar 34: the sharp before the tremolos (D#) and the 8th–rest–16th figures. The program probably took only the stretch between the barlines and dropped the first and last bars. Cue-size notes and the abbreviated tremolo count made the rhythm harder to read. Knowing how many bars the previous line ended with would have settled where this line starts."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_seque